"Positive contraction mappings for classical and quantum Schrödinger systems (https://arxiv.org/html/1405.6650v2)\nciteturn1023view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/1405.6650v2\",\"lineno\":267}); Total lines: 820\nL237: ### III.3 Reduction to the one-step bridge problem\nL238: \nL239: Let $\\Pi(t)=\\left(\\pi_{x_{t},x_{t+1}}(t)\\right)_{x_{t},x_{t+1}=1}^{N}$ and $\\Pi^{\\circ}(t)=\\left(\\pi^{\\circ}_{x_{t},x_{t+1}}(t)\\right)_{x_{t},x_{t+1}=1}^{N}$ be the transition matrices of the prior distribution and of the bridge distribution. It is interesting to express (【53†21】) in matrix form. To this end, we introduce the notation\nL240: \nL241:  | $$\\phi(t)=\\mathop{\\mathrm{diag}}\\left(\\varphi(t,x_{1}),\\varphi(t,x_{2}),\\ldots,\\varphi(t,x_{N})\\right)$$  |\nL242: for a diagonal matrix formed out of the entries of $\\varphi(t,\\cdot)$. We now have\nL243: \nL244:  | $$\\Pi^{\\circ}(t)=\\phi(t)^{-1}\\Pi(t)\\phi(t+1).$$  |  | (22)\nL245: \nL246: Consider now for $n\\geq 1$ $\\Pi^{(n)}(t)=\\left(\\pi^{(n)}_{x_{t},x_{t+n}}(t)\\right)_{x_{t},x_{t+n}=1}^{N}$ the $n$-step transition probabilities matrix. By the Markov property, we have\nL247: \nL248:  | $$\\Pi^{(n)}(t)=\\Pi(t)\\cdot\\Pi(t+1)\\cdots\\Pi(t+n-1).$$  |\nL249: Being the product of stochastic matrices, $\\Pi^{(n)}(t)$ is also stochastic^{3}^{3} 3 The elements of $\\Pi^{(n)}(t)$ are nonnegative as sum of products of nonnegative numbers. Moreover, let $\\mathds{1}^{\\dagger}=(1,1,\\ldots,1)$. Then the fact that a matrix $Q$ has rows summing to one can be expressed as $Q\\mathds{1}=\\mathds{1}$.\nL250: As each $\\Pi(t)$ has rows summing to one , we have $$\\Pi^{(n)}(t)\\mathds{1}=\\Pi(t)\\cdot\\Pi(t+1)\\cdots\\Pi(t+n-1)\\mathds{1}=\\Pi(t)\\cdot\\Pi(t+1)\\cdots\\Pi(t+n-2)\\mathds{1}=\\cdots=\\Pi(t)\\mathds{1}=\\mathds{1}.$$ . Consider now $\\Pi^{\\circ^{(n)}}(t)$. By (【60†22】), we get\nL251:  | $\\displaystyle\\Pi^{\\circ^{(n)}}(t)=\\Pi^{\\circ}(t)\\cdot\\Pi^{\\circ}(t+1)\\cdots\\Pi^{\\circ}(t+n-1)$  |\nL252:  | $\\displaystyle=\\phi(t)^{-1}\\Pi^{(n)}(t)\\phi(t+n).$  |\nL253: \nL254: Thus, we get the following remarkable generalization of formula (【53†21】)\nL255: \nL256:  | $$\\pi_{x_{t}x_{t+n}}^{\\circ^{(n)}}(t)=\\pi^{(n)}_{x_{t},x_{t+n}}(t)\\frac{\\varphi(t+n,x_{t+n})}{\\varphi(t,x_{t})}.$$  |  | (23)\nL257: Consider now $\\Pi^{(T)}(0)$ as the transition matrix of a prior “stroboscopic” evolution. Consider also the Schrödinger bridge problem with the same marginals $\\mathbf{p}_{0}$ and $\\mathbf{p}_{T}$ as before at the two consecutive times $0$ and $T$. Specializing Theorem 【61†2】 to this simple situation we get that the new transition probabilities are precisely given by (【62†23】) with $n=T$ where $\\varphi$, $\\hat{\\varphi}$ satisfy (【43†9】)-(【44†10】)-(【45†11】), namely\nL258:  | $\\displaystyle\\varphi(0,x_{0})$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{x_{T}}\\pi^{(T)}_{x_{0},x_{T}}(0)\\varphi(T,x_{T}),$  |  | (24)\nL259:  | $\\displaystyle\\hat{\\varphi}(T,x_{T})$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i}\\pi^{(T)}_{x_{0},x_{T}}(0)\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0}).$  |  | (25)\nL260: \nL261: with the boundary conditions\nL262:  | $$\\varphi(0,x_{0})\\cdot\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0})=\\mathbf{p}_{0}(x_{0}),\\quad\\varphi(T,x_{T})\\cdot\\hat{\\varphi}(T,x_{T})=\\mathbf{p}_{T}(x_{T}),\\quad\\forall x_{0},x_{T}\\in\\mathcal{X}.$$  |  | (26)\nL263: We see that the solution of Problem 【40†1】 yields, as a by-product, the solution of the one-step problem. The converse, however, is also true. Solving (【63†24】)-(【64†26】), yields the correct terminal value $\\varphi(T,\\cdot)$ from which $\\varphi$ can be computed at all times through the iteration (【50†18】). From it, the new transition probabilities are obtained through (【53†21】).\nL264: We already know from Section 【7†III.1】 that solving the one-step problem suffices to characterize the optimal distribution $Q^{\\circ}$. The argument above shows that it also permits to obtain the more explicit description, namely the transition mechanism of $Q^{\\circ}$, which is desirable in the Markov case.\nL265: ### III.4 The solution to the one-step bridge problem\nL266: \nL267: Motivated by what we have seen in Sections 【7†III.1】 and 【9†III.3】, we study the one-step bridge problem with possibly non Markovian prior. The following result establishes existence and uniqueness for the system (【43†9】)-(【44†10】)-(【45†11】).\nL268: ###### Theorem 3\nL269: \nL270: Given a $N\\times N$ stochastic matrix\nL271: \nL272:  | $$\\Pi=\\left[\\pi_{x_{0},x_{T}}\\right]_{x_{0},x_{T}=1}^{N}$$  |\nL273: \nL274: with strictly positive entries ($\\pi_{x_{0},x_{T}}>0$) and probability distributions ${\\mathbf{p}}_{0}$, ${\\mathbf{p}}_{T}$, there exist four vectors $\\varphi(0,x_{0}),\\,\\varphi(T,x_{T}),\\,\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0}),\\,\\hat{\\varphi}(T,x_{T})$, indexed by $x_{0},x_{T}\\in\\mathcal{X},$ with positive entries such that\nL275:  | $\\displaystyle\\varphi(0,x_{0})$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{x_{T}}\\pi_{x_{0},x_{T}}\\varphi(T,x_{T}),$  |  | (27a)\nL276:  | $\\displaystyle\\hat{\\varphi}(T,x_{T})$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{x_{0}}\\pi_{x_{0},x_{T}}\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0}),$  |  | (27b)\nL277:  | $\\displaystyle\\varphi(0,x_{0})\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0})$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle{\\mathbf{p}}_{0}(x_{0}),$  |  | (27c)\nL278:  | $\\displaystyle\\varphi(T,x_{T})\\hat{\\varphi}(T,x_{T})$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle{\\mathbf{p}}_{T}(x_{T}).$  |  | (27d)\nL279: The four vectors are unique up to multiplication of $\\varphi(0,x_{0})$ and $\\varphi(T,x_{T})$ by the same positive constant and division of $\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0})$ and $\\hat{\\varphi}(T,x_{T})$ by the same constant.\nL280: Rather than relying on the results of Beurling and Jamison as in [【23†22】, 【24†29】], we give an independent proof which also yields an effective algorithm. The proof relies on showing that a certain iteration is strictly contractive in the Hilbert metric^{4}^{4} 4 Fortet’s proof, which we find very difficult to follow, is apparently based on establishing monotonicity of two sequences of functions produced by the iteration..\nL281: ###### Lemma 1\nL282: \nL283: Consider the following circular diagram of maps\nL284:  | $$\\begin{array}[]{ccccc}&\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0})&\\overset{{\\mathcal{E}}^{\\dagger}}{\\longrightarrow}&\\hat{\\varphi}(T,x_{T})&=\\sum_{x_{0}}\\pi_{x_{0},x_{T}}\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0})\\\\\nL285: \\\\\nL286: \\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0})=\\frac{{\\mathbf{p}}_{0}(x_{0})}{\\varphi(0,x_{0})}&\\uparrow&&\\downarrow&\\varphi(T,x_{T})=\\frac{{\\mathbf{p}}_{T}(x_{T})}{\\hat{\\varphi}(T,x_{T})}\\\\\nL287: \\\\\nL288: \\sum_{x_{N}}\\pi_{x_{0},x_{N}}\\varphi(T,x_{T})=&\\varphi(0,x_{0})&\\overset{{\\mathcal{E}}}{\\longleftarrow}&\\varphi(T,x_{T})&\\end{array}$$  |  | (28)\nL289: \nL290: where\nL291:  | $\\displaystyle{\\hat{\\mathcal{D}}}_{0}\\;:\\;\\varphi(0,x_{0})$  | $\\displaystyle\\mapsto$  | $\\displaystyle\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0})=\\frac{{\\mathbf{p}}_{0}(x_{0})}{\\varphi(0,x_{0})}$  |\nL292:  | $\\displaystyle{\\mathcal{D}}_{T}\\;:\\;\\hat{\\varphi}(T,x_{T})$  | $\\displaystyle\\mapsto$  | $\\displaystyle\\varphi(T,x_{T})=\\frac{{\\mathbf{p}}_{T}(x_{N})}{\\hat{\\varphi}(T,x_{T})}$  |\nL293: \nL294: represent componentwise division of vectors. Then the composition\nL295:  | $$\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0})\\overset{{\\mathcal{E}}^{\\dagger}}{\\longrightarrow}\\hat{\\varphi}(T,x_{T})\\overset{{\\mathcal{D}}_{T}}{\\longrightarrow}\\varphi(T,x_{T})\\overset{{\\mathcal{E}}}{\\longrightarrow}\\varphi(0,x_{0})\\overset{{\\mathcal{D}}_{0}}{\\longrightarrow}\\left(\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0})\\right)_{\\rm next}$$  |\nL296: \nL297: is contractive in the Hilbert metric.\nL298: \nL299: Before proceeding with the proof we provide a note about notation: The map\nL300:  | $${\\mathcal{E}}^{\\dagger}\\;:\\;\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0})\\mapsto\\hat{\\varphi}(T,x_{T})=\\sum_{x_{0}}\\pi_{x_{0},x_{N}}\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0})$$  |\nL301: \nL302: is the adjoint of the backward evolution\nL303: \nL304:  | $${\\mathcal{E}}\\;:\\;\\varphi(T,x_{T})\\mapsto\\varphi(0,x_{0})=\\sum_{x_{N}}\\pi_{x_{0},x_{T}}\\varphi(T,x_{T}),$$  |\nL305: \nL306: which is consistent with the standard notation in diffusion processes where the Fokker-Planck (forward) equation involves the adjoint of the generator appearing in the backward Kolmogorov equation.\nL307: Also notice that the componentwise divisions of ${\\hat{\\mathcal{D}}}_{0}$ and ${\\mathcal{D}}_{T}$ are well defined. Indeed, even when $\\hat{\\varphi}(0)$ ($\\varphi(T)$) has zero entries, $\\hat{\\varphi}(T)$ ($\\varphi(0)$) has all positive entries since the elements of $\\Pi$ are all positive.\nL308: \nL309: Proof of Lemma 【65†1】: First note that\nL310:  | $\\displaystyle d_{H}(x,y)$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\log\\frac{\\max_{i}(x_{i}/y_{i})}{\\min_{i}(x_{i}/y_{i})}$  |\nL311:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle d_{H}(z,\\mathds{1})$  |\nL312: \nL313: with $z_{i}=x_{i}/y_{i}$ and $\\mathds{1}$ being the vector with all entries equal to $1$. Since ${\\mathcal{E}}(\\mathds{1})=\\mathds{1}$, the diameter of the range of ${\\mathcal{E}}$ is\nL314:  | $\\displaystyle\\Delta({\\mathcal{E}})$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\sup\\{d_{H}({\\mathcal{E}}(x),\\mathds{1})\\mid x_{i}>0\\}$  |\nL315:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\sup\\{\\log\\left(\\frac{\\pi_{ij}\\pi_{k,\\ell}}{\\pi_{i,\\ell}\\pi_{k,j}}\\right)\\mid 1\\leq i,j,k,\\ell\\leq n\\}.$  |\nL316: \nL317: Thus, the diameter of ${\\mathcal{E}}$ is finite since all entries $\\pi_{i,j}$’s are positive. Birkhoff’s theorem (Theorem 【36†1】) provides a contraction coefficient for linear positive maps and, in this case, we have\nL318:  | $$\\kappa({\\mathcal{E}})=\\tanh(\\frac{1}{4}\\Delta({\\mathcal{E}}))<1.$$  |\nL319: \nL320: For the adjoint map ${\\mathcal{E}}^{\\dagger}$ we only need to note that it is homogeneous of degree $1$ and therefore, by Birkhoff’s theorem,\nL321: \nL322:  | $$\\kappa({\\mathcal{E}}^{\\dagger})\\leq 1.$$  |\nL323: Next we note that both ${\\hat{\\mathcal{D}}}_{0}$ and ${\\mathcal{D}}_{T}$ are isometries in the Hilbert metric since inversion and element-wise scaling are both isometries. Indeed, for vectors $[x_{i}]_{i=1}^{N}$, $[y_{i}]_{i=1}^{N}$, it holds that\nL324:  | $\\displaystyle d_{H}([x_{i}],[y_{i}])$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\log\\left((\\max_{i}(x_{i}/y_{i}))\\frac{1}{\\min_{i}(x_{i}/y_{i})}\\right)$  |\nL325:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\log\\left(\\frac{1}{\\min_{i}((x_{i})^{-1}/(y_{i})^{-1})}\\max_{i}((x_{i})^{-1}/(y_{i})^{-1})\\right)$  |\nL326:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle d_{H}([(x_{i})^{-1}],[(y_{i})^{-1}])$  |\nL327: \nL328: and\nL329:  | $\\displaystyle d_{H}([{\\mathbf{p}}_{i}x_{i}],[{\\mathbf{p}}_{i}y_{i}])$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\log\\frac{\\max_{i}(({\\mathbf{p}}_{i}x_{i})/({\\mathbf{p}}_{i}y_{i}))}{\\min_{i}(({\\mathbf{p}}_{i}x_{i})/({\\mathbf{p}}_{i}y_{i}))}$  |\nL330:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\log\\frac{\\max_{i}(x_{i}/y_{i})}{\\min_{i}(x_{i}/y_{i})}=d_{H}([x_{i}],[y_{i}]).$  |\nL331: \nL332: Finally, we observe that\nL333:  | $$\\kappa({\\hat{\\mathcal{D}}}_{0}\\circ{\\mathcal{E}}\\circ{\\mathcal{D}}_{T}\\circ{\\mathcal{E}}^{\\dagger})\\leq\\kappa({\\hat{\\mathcal{D}}}_{0})\\kappa({\\mathcal{E}})\\kappa({\\mathcal{D}}_{T})\\kappa({\\mathcal{E}}^{\\dagger})<1,$$  |\nL334: \nL335: where $\\circ$ denotes composition. Therefore, the composition ${\\mathcal{C}}:={\\hat{\\mathcal{D}}}_{0}\\circ{\\mathcal{E}}\\circ{\\mathcal{D}}_{T}\\circ{\\mathcal{E}}^{\\dagger}$ is contractive: Namely,\nL336:  | $$d_{H}({\\mathcal{C}}(x),{\\mathcal{C}}(y))\\leq\\kappa({\\mathcal{C}})d_{H}(x,y),\\quad 0\\leq\\kappa({\\mathcal{C}})<1.$$  |\nL337: \nL338: This completes the proof of the lemma. $\\Box$\nL339: Proof of Theorem 【66†3】: By Theorem 3.3 in [【28†6】], which is based on Banach’s contraction mapping theorem, there is a unique positive fixed point $\\hat{\\varphi}(0,\\cdot)=[\\hat{\\varphi}(0,x_{0})]$ of the composition ${\\mathcal{C}}:={\\hat{\\mathcal{D}}}_{0}\\circ{\\mathcal{E}}\\circ{\\mathcal{D}}_{T}\\circ{\\mathcal{E}}^{\\dagger}$. From this we can obtain\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nScaling limit of Sinkhorn-rescaled Random Matrices via Stability of Static Schrödinger Bridges (https://arxiv.org/html/2604.23461v1)\nciteturn1023view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2604.23461v1\",\"lineno\":154}); Total lines: 1520\nL137: ### 2.2. Stability of the static Schrödinger bridge and potentials\nL138: \nL139: In this section, we state three quantitative stability estimates for the static Schrödinger bridge and Schrödinger potentials with respect to perturbations of the kernel and the margins.\nL140: We first formulate our two stability results for the static Schrödinger bridge in a measure-theoretic setting. For a measure space $(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{F},\\mu)$, we write $\\mathcal{P}_{\\geq 0}(\\mathcal{X},\\mu)$ for the set of probability measures that have density with respect to $\\mu$ on $\\mathcal{X}$, and $\\mathcal{P}_{>0}(\\mathcal{X},\\mu)$ for the strictly positive subset, i.e., the set of probability measures equivalent to $\\mu$.\nL141: We fix marginal probability spaces $(\\Omega_{\\mathbf{r}},\\mathcal{F}_{\\mathbf{r}},\\mu)$ and $(\\Omega_{\\mathbf{c}},\\mathcal{F}_{\\mathbf{c}},\\nu)$.\nL142: To formulate our stability results, we consider the family of static Schrödinger bridges $\\pi^{\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1};\\mathcal{R}}$ with marginals $\\mu_{0}\\in\\mathcal{P}_{\\geq 0}(\\Omega_{\\mathbf{r}},\\mu)$, $\\mu_{1}\\in\\mathcal{P}_{\\geq 0}(\\Omega_{\\mathbf{c}},\\nu)$, and reference measure $\\mathcal{R}\\in\\mathcal{P}_{>0}(\\Omega_{\\mathbf{r}}\\otimes\\Omega_{\\mathbf{c}},\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1})$. We then provide bounds on the change in the bridges as we perturb the reference measure or the marginal densities.\nL143: In the discrete case of the matrix scaling problem (see (【35†1.2】)), several necessary and sufficient conditions for the existence of $\\pi^{\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1};\\mathcal{R}}$ are known (see, e.g., Menon and Schneider [【56†33】] and Proposition 【57†5.1】), and the uniqueness follows from the existence.\nL144: In the general continuous setting, a sufficient condition for existence and uniqueness of the bridge is that $\\mathcal{R}$ is measure-theoretically equivalent to $\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1}$ and there is at least one coupling $\\pi\\in\\Pi(\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1})$ with finite KL-divergence to $\\mathcal{R}$ (see [【38†34】, Thm. 2.1]).\nL145: Recall that for two measures $\\mu,\\mu^{\\prime}$ both absolutely continuous with respect to a base measure $\\sigma$, the Hellinger distance is\nL146: \nL147: (2.1)  |  | $\\displaystyle d_{H}(\\mu,\\mu^{\\prime})=\\left(\\int\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{d\\mu}{d\\sigma}}-\\sqrt{\\frac{d\\mu^{\\prime}}{d\\sigma}}\\right)^{2}d\\sigma\\right)^{1/2}$  |\nL148: and is independent of the choice of $\\sigma$. For our purposes, it will be convenient to use either ${\\sigma}=\\mu\\otimes\\nu$ or $\\sigma=\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1}$. We also remind the reader that the Hellinger metric induces the same topology as the total variation/$L_{1}$ metric through the relation\nL149: \nL150: (2.2)  |  | $\\displaystyle d_{H}(\\mu,\\mu^{\\prime})^{2}\\leq d_{TV}(\\mu,\\mu^{\\prime})\\leq 2\\sqrt{2}d_{H}(\\mu,\\mu^{\\prime})$  |\nL151: where $d_{TV}(\\mu,\\mu^{\\prime})=\\int|\\frac{d\\mu}{d\\sigma}-\\frac{d\\mu^{\\prime}}{d\\sigma}|d\\sigma$.\nL152: \nL153: Our first result establishes that, for fixed margins, the map from the reference measure $\\mathcal{R}$ to the bridge $\\pi^{\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1};\\mathcal{R}}$ is Lipschitz continuous in the Hellinger distance.\nL154: ###### Theorem 2.1 (Stability of SSB w.r.t. reference measure).\nL155: \nL156: Fix marginal probability measures $\\mu_{0}\\in\\mathcal{P}_{\\geq 0}(\\Omega_{\\mathbf{r}},\\mu)$ and $\\mu_{1}\\in\\mathcal{P}_{\\geq 0}(\\Omega_{\\mathbf{c}},\\nu)$. Fix reference probability measures $\\mathcal{R},\\mathcal{R}^{\\prime}$ where $d\\mathcal{R}=e^{-\\kappa}d(\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1})$ and $d\\mathcal{R}^{\\prime}=e^{-\\kappa^{\\prime}}d(\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1})$ for some $\\kappa,\\kappa^{\\prime}\\in L^{\\infty}(\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1})$. Then we have\nL157: (2.3)  |  | $\\displaystyle d_{H}(\\pi^{\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1};\\mathcal{R}},\\pi^{\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1};\\mathcal{R}^{\\prime}})\\leq\\exp\\left(\\tfrac{3}{2}\\lVert\\kappa^{+}\\rVert_{\\infty}\\vee\\lVert(\\kappa^{\\prime})^{+}\\rVert_{\\infty}\\right)d_{H}(\\mathcal{R},\\mathcal{R}^{\\prime}).$  |\nL158: A few aspects of the result above are worth noting. Since we assume $\\mathcal{R},\\mathcal{R}^{\\prime}\\sim\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1}$, the bridges $\\pi^{\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1};\\mathcal{R}}$ and $\\pi^{\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1};\\mathcal{R}^{\\prime}}$ share the same support. Comparing them in the Hellinger metric — a strong topology on densities — is therefore natural, in contrast to the weaker topologies (e.g., Wasserstein) used in much of the literature.\nL159: Furthermore, the factor $\\exp\\bigl(\\tfrac{3}{2}\\|\\kappa^{+}\\|_{\\infty}\\vee\\|(\\kappa^{\\prime})^{+}\\|_{\\infty}\\bigr)$ cannot be improved in general: Example 【58†4.4】 exhibits a family of reference measures for which the Lipschitz constant must diverge as the cost grows.\nL160: Since perturbing the reference measure $d\\mathcal{R}=e^{-\\kappa}d(\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1})$ is equivalent to perturbing the cost function $\\kappa$, Theorem 【52†2.1】 can be viewed as a stability result with respect to the cost under fixed marginals. A detailed comparison with existing stability results in the literature is deferred to Section 【14†3.2】.\nL161: \nL162: Our second result controls the bridge under perturbations of the marginals when the kernel is fixed.\nL163: ###### Theorem 2.2 (Stability of SSB w.r.t. margins).\nL164: Fix pairs of marginal probability measures $(\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1})$ and $(\\mu_{0}^{\\prime},\\mu_{1}^{\\prime})$, where $\\frac{d\\mu_{0}}{d\\mu}=\\rho_{\\mathbf{r}}$, $\\frac{d\\mu_{0}^{\\prime}}{d\\mu}=\\rho_{\\mathbf{r}}^{\\prime}$, $\\frac{d\\mu_{1}}{d\\nu}=\\rho_{\\mathbf{c}}$, and $\\frac{d\\mu_{1}^{\\prime}}{d\\nu}=\\rho_{\\mathbf{c}}^{\\prime}$ for some positive probability densities $\\rho_{\\mathbf{r}},\\rho_{\\mathbf{r}}^{\\prime}$, $\\rho_{\\mathbf{c}}$, and $\\rho_{\\mathbf{c}}^{\\prime}$.\nL165: Fix a reference probability measure $\\mathcal{R}$ and suppose $\\mathcal{R}\\sim\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1}$ and $\\mathcal{R}\\sim\\mu_{0}^{\\prime}\\otimes\\mu_{1}^{\\prime}$. Write cost functions $\\kappa,\\kappa^{\\prime}$ as $e^{-\\kappa}=\\frac{d\\mathcal{R}}{d(\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1})}$ and $e^{-\\kappa^{\\prime}}=\\frac{d\\mathcal{R}}{d(\\mu_{0}^{\\prime}\\otimes\\mu_{1}^{\\prime})}$. Then we have\nL166: (2.4)  |  | $\\displaystyle d_{H}(\\pi^{\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1};\\mathcal{R}},\\pi^{\\mu_{0}^{\\prime},\\mu_{1}^{\\prime};\\mathcal{R}})^{2}\\leq 4(\\|\\kappa\\|_{\\infty}\\lor\\|\\kappa^{\\prime}\\|_{\\infty})\\left(\\lVert\\rho_{\\mathbf{r}}-\\rho_{\\mathbf{r}}^{\\prime}\\rVert_{L^{1}(\\mu)}+\\lVert\\rho_{\\mathbf{c}}-\\rho_{\\mathbf{c}}^{\\prime}\\rVert_{L^{1}(\\nu)}\\right).$  |\nL167: Two features of this bound are worth noting. First, the left-hand side is the square of the Hellinger distance, giving Hölder-$1/2$ regularity in the $L^{1}$ distance between the margins. Second, the constant depends only linearly on the cost, milder than the exponential dependence $e^{\\|\\kappa\\|_{\\infty}}$ in the reference stability (Theorem 【52†2.1】).\nL168: By combining Theorems 【52†2.1】 and 【53†2.2】 via the triangle inequality, we obtain total stability under simultaneous perturbation of both the kernel and the margins.\nL169: ###### Theorem 2.3 (Total stability of SSB).\nL170: \nL171: Make the same assumptions as in Theorems 【52†2.1】 and 【53†2.2】. Let $d\\mathcal{R}=e^{-\\kappa}d(\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1})$ and $d\\mathcal{R}^{\\prime}=e^{-\\kappa^{\\prime}}d(\\mu_{0}^{\\prime}\\otimes\\mu_{1}^{\\prime})$. Further assume that\nL172: \nL173: (2.5)  |  | $\\displaystyle e^{-M}\\leq\\frac{d(\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1})}{d(\\mu_{0}^{\\prime}\\otimes\\mu_{1}^{\\prime})}\\leq e^{M}\\hskip 10.00002pt\\mu\\otimes\\nu\\text{ a.s. }$  |\nL174: \nL175: for some $M\\geq 0$. Then\nL176: (2.6)  |  |  | $\\displaystyle d_{H}(\\pi^{\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1};\\mathcal{R}},\\pi^{\\mu_{0}^{\\prime},\\mu_{1}^{\\prime};\\mathcal{R}^{\\prime}})^{2}$  |\nL177: (2.7)  |  |  | $\\displaystyle\\hskip 20.00003pt\\leq 8(\\lVert\\kappa\\rVert_{\\infty}+M)\\left(\\lVert\\rho_{\\mathbf{r}}-\\rho_{\\mathbf{r}}^{\\prime}\\rVert_{L^{1}(\\mu)}+\\lVert\\rho_{\\mathbf{c}}-\\rho_{\\mathbf{c}}^{\\prime}\\rVert_{L^{1}(\\nu)}\\right)+4e^{3(\\lVert\\kappa\\rVert_{\\infty}+M)\\vee\\lVert(\\kappa^{\\prime})\\rVert_{\\infty}}\\,d_{H}(\\mathcal{R},\\mathcal{R}^{\\prime})^{2}.$  |\nL178: ###### Remark 2.4 (Role of the constant $M$).\nL179: The constant $M$ in Theorem 【54†2.3】 bounds the Radon–Nikodym derivative $d(\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1})/d(\\mu_{0}^{\\prime}\\otimes\\mu_{1}^{\\prime})$ and appears solely to handle a change-of-reference step in the triangle inequality combining Theorems 【52†2.1】 and 【53†2.2】. In the scaling limit theory of Section 【10†2.4】, where $\\delta$-smoothness (see Def.\nL180: 【59†2.7】) makes marginal densities uniformly comparable, $M$ is absorbed into constants depending only on $\\delta$, and does not affect the asymptotic convergence rates.\nL181: This total stability estimate is the main tool in our scaling limit theory: it allows us to control the convergence of a sequence of discrete Schrödinger bridges (with converging margins and kernels) to the continuous SSB.\nL182: The kernel and margin stability results above (Theorems 【52†2.1】 and 【53†2.2】) control the coupling $\\pi$ in the Hellinger metric. While these results are sufficient for establishing scaling limits, the analysis of finite-dimensional Sinkhorn-rescaled random matrices requires a complementary result that controls the discrete Schrödinger potentials (the logarithms of the scaling factors) in the $L^{\\infty}$ norm, and that remains valid when the input matrix may have zero entries.\nL183: The following theorem provides such a stability bound. The closest precedent is [【60†28】, Lem. 9], which establishes an $\\ell^{\\infty}$ stability bound for the scaling factors under approximate scaling by extending the uniqueness argument of Sinkhorn and Knopp [【29†40】]; however, the constants there scale with the ratio of the maximum to the minimum entry of the input matrix, and therefore degenerate whenever any entry vanishes.\nL184: Our result instead relies on a row-alignment parameter that measures, in aggregate across columns, the inner-product overlap between pairs of rows. This condition admits matrices with many zero entries, and ensures that the Sinkhorn scaling map remains a well-conditioned local diffeomorphism, allowing the potentials to be controlled via a Banach fixed-point argument on its linearization at the origin.\nL185: This is the key technical ingredient for the concentration theory for rescaled random matrices stated in Section 【9†2.3】.\nL186: ###### Theorem 2.5 (Stability of discrete Schrödinger potentials).\nL187: Let $\\mathbf{A}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}$ be a matrix with non-negative entries, and let $\\mathbf{r}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}_{>0}$ and $\\mathbf{c}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}_{>0}$ be target margin vectors with strictly positive entries such that $\\|\\mathbf{r}\\|_{1}=\\|\\mathbf{c}\\|_{1}=N$. Let $\\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{A})=\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{1}_{n}$ and $\\mathbf{c}(\\mathbf{A})=\\mathbf{A}^{T}\\mathbf{1}_{m}$ denote the current row and column margins of $\\mathbf{A}$.\n"
